In this lesson we'll look at two simple rules that solve almost every counting problem you'll meet. They're called the multiplication principle and the addition principle, and the trick is just knowing which one to reach for. First, the multiplication principle. Whenever a task is made up of stages, and you do them one after another, you multiply the number of choices at each stage. So if you're picking an outfit, and you have three shirts and four pairs of pants, you do three times four, which gives you twelve possible outfits. See the pattern? "And then" means multiply. Next, the addition principle. This one kicks in when your options are mutually exclusive, meaning you pick exactly one from a set of separate choices. For example, if you're traveling somewhere and you can either catch one of five buses or one of eight trains, you just add them: five plus eight gives you thirteen options. So whenever you hear "or" between choices that can't both happen, you add them up. The big takeaway is simple: "and then" means multiply, "or" means add. Now let's see it in action.